    "explanation": "To both parties: this dispute cannot be decided on the record before me, so under POL-RD-10 it goes to a human reviewer. The escalation is terminal: the disputed SGD 18.56 is held, not collected or refunded, and no money moves now.\n\nWHAT IS AGREED. All three instrument artifacts concern the same trip, TRP-00512, and the GPS trace's first fix at 19:16:40 matches the trip record's started_at, so the rider has shown her POL-RD-08(a) same-trip condition. The amount charged was SGD 18.56.\n\nTHE RIDER'S EXCESS. On the trace figures the trip ran 8.92 km against a booked optimal of 6.1 km — an excess of 2.82 km, or 46.23% of optimal — which clears both POL-RD-01 gates (more than 0.8 km and more than 20%).\n\nWHY THAT CANNOT DECIDE THE CASE. EV-GPS-00512 (GPS_TRACE, weight 0.39, issue codes GPS_GAP and LOW_COVERAGE, coverage 44.9%) is impaired. It is impaired at BOTH levels: at the schema level, and for policy purposes, because it contains a single 860-second gap between consecutive fixes (19:23:30 to 19:37:50) that exceeds the 90-second limb (a) of POL-RD-07. Under POL-RD-07(ii) a deviation may not be established on an impaired trace alone; it needs one of a short list of independent sources, and none is present: the chat log (EV-CHAT-00512) has zero messages, so there is no driver admission in it; the fare breakdown (EV-FARE-00512) charges no ERP gantries, so no gantry places the vehicle on a corridor the optimal route avoids; the billed distance on the trip record and the ledger (8.9 km) is the rounded copy of the same trace measurement — POL-RD-01 says so and POL-RD-07(ii) refuses it as independent corroboration; and the driver's statement ('my phone hung, so I drove from memory') explains why navigation was lost but does not admit the route — it names no road, corridor or waypoint and concedes no identified trajectory, so it is not a counterparty_statement admitting the route and cannot carry the 2.82 km excess. The rider's claim that the driver's statement supplies the independent corroboration is rejected for that reason.\n\nTHE TRIGGERS. POL-RD-10(3) fires: the trace is impaired under POL-RD-07(a)-(c) and no corroborating artifact of the kind POL-RD-07(ii) names exists. POL-RD-10(1) fires independently: the deciding fact — the excess — is carried only by the impaired trace at weight 0.39, because the trip record and the fare ledger rest on the same measurement and are not independent carriers (POL-GEN-02(2)); the counterparty statement carries no weight and does not lift the cap. A cap of 0.39 is below the platform floor of 0.70.\n\nTHE MONEY THAT WOULD HAVE FOLLOWED. Had the deviation been established, POL-RD-02 would recompute the fare as ((3.80 base + 0.75 × 6.1 optimal km + 0.28 × 11.0 optimal min) × the 1.0 surge multiplier read from the fare breakdown) + 0.80 platform fee = 12.255, giving 18.56 − 12.255 = 6.305, rounded up to SGD 6.35 (33.97% of the charge, above the SGD 0.50 de minimis and below the 90% full-refund share). I record that arithmetic so the reviewer and the rider can see it; it is not applied, because the excess is not established on this record.\n\nOTHER POINTS ANSWERED. The deviation, even at 46.23%, is not MATERIAL (below both the 50% and the 8.0 km gates), so POL-RD-06 is not engaged and no conduct referral, uplift or profile-based finding arises — the driver's profile (0 deviation flags in 90 days over 250 completed trips) would show no pattern in any event. There is no billing-integrity line to refund under POL-RD-09(4): no ERP, no tolls, no airport surcharge and no cancellation fee. The traffic advisory EV-ADVIS-00512-01 is MISSING at weight 0.0 with issue MISSING_ARTIFACT, so POL-RD-03 can be run for neither party, and the driver's account of the trip finds no support in the empty chat log.\n\nWHAT WILL RESOLVE IT. An independent record of the route across 19:23:30–19:37:50: a driver chat admission or waypoint, a corrected or second-source trace, gantry/toll data on the corridor, or a platform-derived trip distance independent of this trace. The reviewer should have the full case summary, both advocate briefs and the admissibility report (POL-RD-10)."
  },
